```
ROLE: area_chair
FINAL_VERDICT: yellow
REVIEWER_VOTES: {novelty_killer: yellow, systems_reviewer: yellow, evaluation_prosecutor: yellow, theory_skeptic: yellow, product_realist: yellow}

FATAL_OBJECTIONS: []

REQUIRED_EVIDENCE: [
  "1. Lossless-re-encode prevalence in real VLM-serving or agent-RAG traffic from a NAMED source (production trace, WildVision, LMSYS multimodal arena, or instrumented client SDK pipeline) — not a hand-curated transform list. This is the single gate: if lossless fraction < ~5% of real requests, the 100pp gap is mechanically correct but operationally trivial and the claim weakens to a footnote.",
  "2. FLOPs and/or TTFT saved per recovered hit under realistic working-set pressure, including processed-tensor cache memory overhead (decoded float tensors are ~10-50x larger per entry than compressed transport bytes — SGLang's advantage may partially erode under memory-constrained eviction).",
  "3. Reverse-confound test: processor-version drift (Qwen2VLImageProcessor patch upgrade) silently invalidating processed-tensor hashes while byte hashes survive. If this is nonzero, the cross-framework comparison is confounded in the other direction.",
  "4. Baseline: vLLM with client-injected mm_uuid on the same traffic to measure how much of the gap the existing escape hatch already closes when actually used.",
  "5. At least one additional framework (HuggingFace TGI or TensorRT-LLM) checked for byte-vs-processed keying to establish whether byte-keying is an industry-wide default or a vLLM-specific quirk."
]

MANDATORY_BASELINES: [
  "vLLM with client-injected mm_uuid (existing mitigation, measures org-seam cost)",
  "HuggingFace TGI multimodal cache keying (establishes industry baseline)",
  "SGLang under memory-constrained eviction (processed-tensor entries are larger — does eviction erase the hit-rate advantage?)"
]

MAP_DELTA_PROPOSAL: {
  node: "CLAIM-0060",
  change: "HOLD at L0-complete / L1-gated. Tag contribution_type = 'empirical_measurement_of_known_mechanism' (not 'new_failure_mode'). L1 scope: items 1-5 from REQUIRED_EVIDENCE. Advance beyond L1 ONLY if lossless-re-encode prevalence ≥ 5% in a named real-traffic source AND net FLOPs/TTFT gain survives memory-pressure and reverse-confound tests. If prevalence < 5%, converge as 'confirmed-but-marginal' with no further escalation."
}

PRIOR_ART_ADEQUATE: no

RATIONALE:
Unanimous 5/5 yellow — and unusually, the five reviewers converge on the SAME bottleneck rather than flagging orthogonal issues, which strengthens the yellow rather than suggesting disagreement that could be resolved by weighing evidence.

THE MECHANISM IS REAL AND THE L0 IS CLEAN. The cross-framework divergence is a verifiable code-level fact: vLLM hasher.py keys on transport bytes (with mm_processor_cache ON by default, 4GB LRU), SGLang mm_utils.py hash_feature keys on processed pixel_values tensors. On genuine lossless re-encodes (PNG re-opt, EXIF strip, PNG→lossless-WebP, client TIFF re-save), vLLM hits 0%, SGLang hits 100%, pixel-identity verified at max_abs_diff==0. The lossy null-exit holds honestly (JPEG q90/q95, resize → 0pp gap, both correctly miss). The strict-gate miss (all-lossless 16.7% vs pre-registered ≤10%) is NOT disqualifying: the 16.7% is driven solely by L4_base64_rewrap which is byte-identical and correctly hits — it is not a false miss but a non-re-encode. The genuine-re-encode denominator (0%) is the mechanistically correct one and clears the gate decisively. Transparent reporting of both denominators is noted as a positive methodological signal.

THE MECHANISM IS NOT NOVEL. All five reviewers agree, and the packet itself concedes this: cache-key over-specification ("key domain is too broad for the equivalence class") is pre-2020 systems folklore (RFC 7234 cache-key semantics, perceptual hashing, content-addressable dedup, build-system canonicalization). The "fix" — hash the processed tensor — already ships in SGLang. vLLM's own mm_uuid/EXIF-ImageID override is smoking-gun evidence that maintainers already know byte-hashing is fragile. The defensible contribution is therefore EMPIRICAL, not ALGORITHMIC: a measured, verbatim-transcribed, pixel-identity-verified cross-framework divergence with a clean null-exit. That is a valid contribution but must be framed as "quantified a known design-point mismatch" not "discovered a new failure mode." Any L1 framing it as discovery will be correctly attacked.

PRIOR-ART SEARCH IS INADEQUATE. The novelty_killer explicitly reports that live web-search tools were denied, preventing independent verification of the "no prior paper/framework-doc names this as a hit-rate ceiling" claim. Per area-chair rules, GREEN requires adequate prior-art search. The packet's own citations (vLLM hasher.py, SGLang mm_utils.py, mm_uuid override) are code-level, not literature-level. The novelty_killer identified five relevant prior-work classes (pHash/CAS, RFC 7234, GPTCache, vLLM mm_uuid, SGLang hash_feature) from recall alone but could not search for a paper that already measures the cross-framework gap — this gap in verification is itself a reason to hold at yellow. L1 must include a proper literature search with ≥2 independent sources confirming the prior-art negative.

THE DECISIVE QUESTION IS UNMEASURED. Every reviewer independently identifies the same bottleneck: the 100pp gap is conditional on lossless pixel-preserving re-encoding occurring, and the L0 provides zero production telemetry on its prevalence. The theory_skeptic correctly notes the 100pp is the DEFINITIONAL consequence of keying one canonicalization step too early — it is what basic cache-key theory predicts and therefore not surprising when measured. The product_realist notes that dominant CDN traffic is lossy (JPEG re-compression, resize for bandwidth) — the zero-gap arm. The evaluation_prosecutor flags that the 5 genuine re-encode types are hand-curated, not sampled from a traffic distribution. The question is binary: if lossless re-encodes are common (≥5-10% of real VLM/agent-RAG requests — plausible for agent pipelines that hold PIL objects and re-serialize, or privacy-motivated EXIF stripping), the seam is material and an L1 measuring FLOPs/TTFT is warranted. If they are rare (<5%), the seam is real-but-trivial and the claim converges as a confirmed curiosity.

STRICT-GATE RULING: The base64-rewrap-driven 16.7% is a correct hit on a non-re-encode and should not count against the gate. The genuine-re-encode 0% is the right denominator. Gate passes on the mechanistically relevant subset.

ORG-SEAM IS REAL BUT NOT DISQUALIFYING for L0→L1. The mm_uuid fix requires the source/client to inject a stable UUID that survives the re-encode boundary — the serving team cannot fix unilaterally. This is a real adoption barrier (product_realist) but strengthens the case that the gap persists in practice even though maintainers know about it: knowing ≠ fixing when the fix requires cross-org coordination.

ADVANCE CONDITIONS FOR L1: All five items in REQUIRED_EVIDENCE must be scoped into L1. The L1 is an empirical prevalence + cost measurement, NOT a mechanism discovery. If L1 confirms prevalence ≥ 5% in named real traffic AND net FLOPs/TTFT gain survives memory-pressure and reverse-confound tests, advance further. If prevalence < 5%, converge as confirmed-but-marginal. Prompt version v001.
```
